@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{quotidian.el} shows quotations drawn from a corpus of your own choosing.  Quotations live in plain JSON files, so the corpus is yours to extend, and the package reads them once per Emacs session and keeps them in memory.  A small corpus of public-domain quotations is bundled with the package (@ref{Quotations and copyright}).

There are two ways to read them:

@itemize
@item
@strong{In the echo area}, one quotation at a time, while you wait for something else to finish.  The command @code{quotidian-echo} writes a quotation where Emacs writes its own messages, without adding it to @samp{*Messages*}, and is meant to be called from a hook that fires when a long operation starts (@ref{Showing a quotation in the echo area}).
@item
@strong{In a review buffer}, one quotation to a screen, centred horizontally and vertically in a frame with no other window.  The command @code{quotidian} opens it, and single keys move through the quotations, copy them, and leave (@ref{Reading quotations in a review buffer}).
@end itemize

The two entry points share one corpus, one formatter, and three faces, so a quotation looks the same wherever it appears: the text first, then the author and date, then the source on an indented line of its own (@ref{Faces}).

The package requires Emacs 29.1 or later and depends only on libraries bundled with Emacs: @samp{cl-lib}, @samp{seq}, @samp{subr-x}, and the built-in JSON parser.

The development repository is on @uref{https://github.com/benthamite/quotidian, GitHub}.

@menu
* Quotations and copyright::
@end menu

@node Quotations and copyright
@section Quotations and copyright

The package ships public-domain quotations only.  The bundled corpus, @samp{data/quotidian-sample.json}, holds twenty of them.

A dictionary of quotations that is still under copyright cannot be redistributed here, and none of its contents are.  If you want to read from one, transcribe the material into a corpus file of your own, keep that file private, and add it to @code{quotidian-corpus-files} (@ref{Corpus files}).  The package treats your files and the bundled one alike.

