@node File format
@section File format

A corpus file is a JSON array of objects, one object per quotation:

@example
[
  @{
    "text": "The unexamined life is not worth living.",
    "author": "Socrates",
    "source": "Plato, Apology 38a",
    "date": "399 BC",
    "tags": ["philosophy"]
  @}
]
@end example

@multitable {aaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Required
@tab Type
@tab Meaning
@item @samp{text}
@tab yes
@tab non-empty string
@tab The quotation itself
@item @samp{author}
@tab yes
@tab non-empty string
@tab Who said or wrote it
@item @samp{source}
@tab no
@tab string
@tab Where the quotation is from
@item @samp{date}
@tab no
@tab string
@tab When it was said or written
@item @samp{tags}
@tab no
@tab list
@tab Category strings
@end multitable

Both @samp{date} and @samp{tags} are free-form: @samp{date} is a string, not a number, so @samp{399 BC} and @samp{1777} are equally acceptable, but @samp{1777} without the quotation marks is not.  The package keeps @samp{tags} on each quotation but nothing reads it yet; it is there for filtering that has not been written.

@node Entries and files that are rejected
@section Entries and files that are rejected

An entry is dropped, and the rest of the file is kept, when any of the following holds:

@itemize
@item
@samp{text} is missing, is not a string, or is the empty string.
@item
@samp{author} is missing, is not a string, or is the empty string.
@item
@samp{source} or @samp{date} is present but is not a string.
@item
@samp{tags} is present but is not a list.
@end itemize

A whole file is skipped, and none of its quotations are read, for any of three reasons:

@itemize
@item
It cannot be read: it is missing, it is not readable, or it fails for some other file error.
@item
Its contents are not valid JSON@.
@item
Its contents are valid JSON but not an array — a bare number or string, or an object.
@end itemize

The last of those is worth a word, because wrapping the array in an object is a natural first attempt:

@example
@{ "quotes": [ @{ "text": "…", "author": "…" @} ] @}
@end example

That file is skipped whole, with the warning @samp{Corpus file is a JSON object, not an array, skipping it}.  The entries inside are never looked at and are not counted as dropped, since there is nothing wrong with them: the outer shape is the mistake.  Remove the wrapper and leave the array at the top level.

@node Warnings and reloading
@section Warnings and reloading

@findex quotidian-reload

Neither kind of failure signals an error and neither interrupts what you were doing.  Both are logged to @samp{*Warnings*}, quietly: the buffer is written to but never popped up.  A skipped file is logged with its name and the reason; dropped entries are logged as @strong{one line per file}, giving the number dropped and the file they came from, not the entries themselves.  To find the offending entry you must inspect the file.

The corpus is read on first use and then cached for the rest of the session.  If you edit a corpus file, or add one to @code{quotidian-corpus-files}, call @samp{M-x quotidian-reload}.  It discards the cache, reads the files again, and reports the outcome in the echo area:

@example
Quotidian: 20 quotations, 0 files skipped, 0 entries dropped
@end example

Since the counts are the visible half of a log that never shows itself, @code{quotidian-reload} is also the way to check on a corpus you have just written: a number of dropped entries greater than zero tells you to read @samp{*Warnings*}.

@node User options
@chapter User options

All user options belong to the @code{quotidian} customization group.  You can browse them interactively with @samp{M-x customize-group RET quotidian RET}.

@menu
* The corpus files::
* The height of the echo area::
* The width of the review buffer::
@end menu

@node The corpus files
@section The corpus files

@vindex quotidian-corpus-files

The user option @code{quotidian-corpus-files} is the list of files holding quotations, read in order.  An element is either the name of a file or the symbol @code{bundled}, which stands for the corpus file shipped with the package, @samp{data/quotidian-sample.json} (@ref{The @samp{data} directory is part of the package, , The data directory is part of the package}).  The default value is the one-element list @code{(bundled)}.

The symbol is there so that the default holds no absolute path.  A path would be written to your custom file the first time you customized the option, and under a package manager that puts the version number in the installation directory — @samp{package.el} does — the next upgrade would leave it pointing at a directory that no longer exists, and the bundled quotations would quietly disappear.  The symbol is resolved afresh every time the corpus is read.

Add your own files to the list to read more than the bundled twenty:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'quotidian-corpus-files "~/quotations/oxford.json" t)
@end lisp

Every file is read and the quotations are pooled; a quotation is then chosen from the pool at random, so a file ten times the size of another contributes ten times as often.  The draw is with replacement, and the package keeps no record of what you have already been shown, so the same quotation can come up twice in a row; on a small corpus it will, often.  Removing @code{bundled} from the list leaves only your own.  Changing the option takes effect after @code{quotidian-reload} (@ref{Warnings and reloading}).

@node The height of the echo area
@section The height of the echo area

@vindex quotidian-echo-max-lines

The user option @code{quotidian-echo-max-lines} is the greatest height, in lines, of a quotation shown in the echo area.  The value is a non-negative integer and the default is @samp{4}.

A quotation that needs more lines than this is passed over: @code{quotidian-echo} draws another one instead of shortening the one it drew (@ref{Showing a quotation in the echo area}).  Raise the option to see long quotations, at the price of a mini-window that jumps up four or five lines while you are working; lower it to @samp{2} to keep the interruption to the size of an ordinary message.

Emacs will not necessarily give you the height you ask for.  The echo area cannot grow past what @code{resize-mini-windows} and @code{max-mini-window-height} allow, and the package uses whichever of the two limits is smaller, so a quotation it decides to show is never cut off.  On a short frame, or with @code{resize-mini-windows} set to @code{nil} — which pins the echo area to a single line — the effective limit is below @code{quotidian-echo-max-lines} and short quotations are the only ones you will see.

@node The width of the review buffer
@section The width of the review buffer

@vindex quotidian-review-fill-column

The user option @code{quotidian-review-fill-column} is the width, in columns, at which quotations are wrapped in the review buffer.  The value is a non-negative integer and the default is @samp{64}.

This is a measure of line length for reading, not a use of the frame's width: the wrapped block is centred in the window, so a wide frame gives wide margins rather than long lines.  The option is defined in @samp{quotidian-review.el} and has no effect on @code{quotidian-echo}, which wraps to the width of the echo area instead.

@node Faces
@chapter Faces

@vindex quotidian-quote
@vindex quotidian-author
@vindex quotidian-source

Three faces style the parts of a quotation, in the echo area and the review buffer alike.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Face
@tab Inherits from
@tab Applies to
@item @code{quotidian-quote}
@tab @code{default}
@tab The text of the quotation
@item @code{quotidian-author}
@tab @code{font-lock-string-face}
@tab The author, and the date if any
@item @code{quotidian-source}
@tab @code{shadow}
@tab The source, if any
@end multitable

The defaults inherit rather than set attributes, so they follow your theme.  Customize them when you want the attribution to recede further, or the text of the quotation to stand out:

@lisp
(custom-set-faces
 '(quotidian-quote ((t :slant italic))))
@end lisp

Prefer attributes that leave the character grid alone.  Both the line counting that decides whether a quotation fits in the echo area and the centring in the review buffer measure text in columns, so a face that changes the height or the width of a character makes those measurements approximate.

