@node Showing a quotation in the echo area
@section Showing a quotation in the echo area

@findex quotidian-echo

The command @code{quotidian-echo} shows one quotation, chosen at random, in the echo area.  It is written to be called from a hook that fires when you are about to wait — a compilation, a long @code{shell-command}, a package update:

@lisp
(add-hook 'compilation-start-hook (lambda (_process) (quotidian-echo)))
@end lisp

Because it is meant to run unattended, the command is quiet in every failing case rather than signalling.  It does nothing at all when the minibuffer is in use, so it cannot overwrite a prompt you are answering; it does nothing when the corpus is empty; and it does nothing when no quotation fits.  In all cases it returns @code{nil}, which makes it safe on a hook whose return value matters.

Fitting is decided by drawing.  The command draws a quotation at random, wraps it to the width of the echo area, and counts the display lines the result occupies — counting a line too wide to fit as the several lines Emacs will wrap it into.  If the count is within the limit described under @code{quotidian-echo-max-lines} (@ref{The height of the echo area}), that quotation is shown; otherwise the command draws again, up to twenty times, and then gives up silently.  Nothing is ever truncated, and nothing is ever abbreviated with an ellipsis: a quotation either appears whole or does not appear.

The quotation is not written to @samp{*Messages*}.  This is deliberate: a quotation shown once while you waited should not push your real messages out of the log.

Malformed corpus entries cannot make this command signal, because entries are validated when the corpus is read, not when a quotation is displayed (@ref{Entries and files that are rejected}).

@node Reading quotations in a review buffer
@section Reading quotations in a review buffer

@findex quotidian
@findex quotidian-review-mode

The command @code{quotidian} opens the @samp{*quotidian*} buffer, deletes the other windows, and draws a quotation in the middle of the frame: wrapped at @code{quotidian-review-fill-column} (@ref{The width of the review buffer}), centred horizontally, and padded from the top so that it sits at the vertical centre.  The buffer is in @code{quotidian-review-mode}, a mode derived from @code{special-mode}; the cursor is hidden, and the key hints occupy the mode line rather than a line of buffer text.  Resizing the window redraws the quotation at the new size.

The command signals a @code{user-error} if the corpus holds no quotations, since there is nothing to open the buffer for.  A corpus that reads as empty under a package manager is usually the missing @samp{data} directory (@ref{The @samp{data} directory is part of the package, , The data directory is part of the package}).

Quotations you have seen are kept in a history, one per review buffer, so moving forwards and backwards walks the same sequence rather than drawing at random each time.  Each fresh draw is nevertheless independent of the ones before it, so the history is not a tour of the corpus without repeats: a quotation can be drawn again while it is still in the history, and on a small corpus it soon will be.  Calling @code{quotidian} again while the review buffer is already open adds a new quotation to the end of that history and shows it; it does not reset the history, and it does not overwrite the window layout the buffer will restore.

@menu
* Key bindings::
@end menu

@node Key bindings
@subsection Key bindings

@findex quotidian-review-next
@findex quotidian-review-previous
@findex quotidian-review-copy
@findex quotidian-review-quit
@vindex quotidian-review-mode-map

The keymap @code{quotidian-review-mode-map} binds five keys:

@multitable {aaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@tab Description
@item @samp{n}
@tab @code{quotidian-review-next}
@tab Show the next quotation
@item @samp{SPC}
@tab @code{quotidian-review-next}
@tab The same, under the thumb
@item @samp{p}
@tab @code{quotidian-review-previous}
@tab Show the quotation seen before this one
@item @samp{w}
@tab @code{quotidian-review-copy}
@tab Copy the quotation and its attribution
@item @samp{q}
@tab @code{quotidian-review-quit}
@tab Leave, restoring the previous window layout
@end multitable

Every other key keeps the binding @code{special-mode} gives it.  One of those is worth knowing: @samp{g} runs @code{revert-buffer}, and the mode makes that draw the current quotation again rather than fail for want of a file to revert.  Use it after changing @code{quotidian-review-fill-column} (@ref{The width of the review buffer}) to see the new width without moving on to another quotation.

The command @code{quotidian-review-next} steps forward through the history when there is anything ahead, and draws a new quotation at random when there is not.  It signals a @code{user-error} if the corpus has become empty since the buffer was opened — if you emptied @code{quotidian-corpus-files} and reloaded, for instance — rather than showing an empty screen.

The command @code{quotidian-review-previous} steps back one quotation.  At the oldest quotation in the history it reports @samp{No earlier quotation} and stays where it is.

The command @code{quotidian-review-copy} copies the current quotation and its attribution to the kill ring, without text properties.  What you get is the quotation as @code{quotidian-format} builds it (@ref{Formatting a quotation}) — neither wrapped nor centred, unlike the buffer you are looking at — so that it lands in whatever you paste it into at that text's own width.  With a prefix argument (@samp{C-u w}) it copies the text of the quotation alone, which is what you want when you are quoting inside your own sentence.  It reports @samp{Copied} either way.

The command @code{quotidian-review-quit} kills the review buffer and puts the windows back as they were before the review began.  The layout is restored by a @code{kill-buffer-hook}, so it is restored equally when you kill the buffer some other way — @samp{C-x k}, @code{kill-some-buffers}, or a session cleanup — as long as the buffer is on screen at the time.  Killing a review buffer that is not displayed, from the buffer list say, leaves your windows alone: the layout the buffer remembers is the one the review replaced, and by then you are working in a later one.  The command refuses, with a @code{user-error}, if it is somehow called outside a review buffer.

@node Reloading the corpus
@section Reloading the corpus

@findex quotidian-reload

The command @code{quotidian-reload} discards the cached quotations, reads @code{quotidian-corpus-files} again, and reports how many quotations were loaded, how many files were skipped, and how many entries were dropped.  Call it after editing a corpus file, after adding one to the option, and whenever the number of quotations on offer surprises you (@ref{Warnings and reloading}).

