@node Formatting a quotation
@section Formatting a quotation

@findex quotidian-format

The function @code{quotidian-format} takes a quotation — a plist with the keys @code{:text} and @code{:author}, and optionally @code{:source} and @code{:date} — and returns it as a propertized string, faced as described under @ref{Faces}.  The text comes first; the author, followed by the date if there is one, occupies the next line after an em dash; the source, if there is one, occupies a third line, indented by two spaces:

@example
The unexamined life is not worth living.
— Socrates, 399 BC
  Plato, Apology 38a
@end example

The string is unwrapped: the caller decides the width.  This is the function to call if you want to show a quotation somewhere the package does not, such as a dashboard or a scratch buffer, and it is what @code{quotidian-review-copy} copies (@ref{Key bindings}).

@node Running the tests
@chapter Running the tests

The tests are an ERT suite in @samp{test/quotidian-test.el}, with its fixtures in @samp{test/fixtures/}.  Run it from the root of a clone:

@example
emacs -Q --batch -L . -l test/quotidian-test.el -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit
@end example

Nothing is needed beyond Emacs itself: ERT is bundled, no test runs over the network, and @samp{-Q} keeps your configuration out of the result.  The command prints one line per test and exits with a non-zero status if any of them fails.

